            "paragraph": "The methodology to derive particulate matter (PM) emission factors and calculate emissions relies on the methods documented in (Klimont et al., 2002b; Kupiainen and Klimont, 2004, 2007). However, apart from updates to emission factors a number of modifications and extensions have been introduced subsequently, especially for carbonaceous particles. We summarize the principles below, allocating more space to discuss extensions. The emissions of PM in the GAINS model are calculated for several size classes: a submicron fraction (particles with diameter smaller than 1 \u00b5m; \u2264 PM\u2081), a fine fraction (PM2.5), a coarse fraction (>PM2.5, <PM10), and large particles (> PM10). PM10 is calculated as the sum of fine and coarse fractions, total suspended particles (TSP) as the sum of fine, coarse, and \u2265 PM10 fractions. Additionally, black carbon (BC) and organic carbon (OC) are calculated.",
